    <body>
      <sec id="article-29623.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Subdural hematoma results from bleeding beneath the dura mater, most commonly due to rupture of bridging veins exposed to shearing forces. This activity provides an overview of subdural hematoma, emphasizing its anatomical basis, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and management across the lifespan. The activity reviews how age-related brain characteristics, trauma, and abusive head injury&#x02014;particularly in infants and young children&#x02014;contribute to disease development, progression, and complications such as increased intracranial pressure, herniation, seizures, and long-term neurodevelopmental impairment.</p>
        <p>Participants will also gain an understanding&#x000a0;of meningeal anatomy, mechanisms of injury, and risk factors for acute, subacute, and chronic subdural hematomas, as well as the clinical findings, appropriate laboratory and imaging evaluation, and principles of stabilization and treatment for this condition, including indications for neurosurgical intervention.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying subdural hematoma, recognizing inconsistent histories suggestive of abusive head trauma, performing the recommended evaluation, anticipating short- and long-term complications, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical presentations that raise concern for abusive head trauma in patients with subdural hematoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based principles of initial management for patients with suspected subdural hematoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine indications for neurosurgical operative management for patients with subdural hematoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional team members to implement coordinated management strategies that optimize outcomes for patients with subdural hematoma.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29623&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29623">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>A subdural hematoma develops from the accumulation of blood beneath the dura mater, one of the protective layers covering the brain tissue under the calvarium (see <bold>Image</bold>. Subdural Hematoma&#x000a0;Tentorium). An accurate understanding of subdural hematoma depends on knowledge of the neuroanatomical coverings of the brain. The brain serves as the central repository of delicate neural tissue, composed of interconnected neurons and supporting connective tissue that remain highly susceptible to injury without adequate protection. Protection begins with the scalp and bony skull and continues internally with the meninges, which consist of 3 distinct layers.</p>
        <p>Along the inner surface of the skull, the dura mater forms a dense, leather-like layer derived from the neural crest and adheres to the periosteum while facing the arachnoid mater. The arachnoid mater lies beneath the dura as the middle meningeal layer and forms numerous villi that penetrate the dura. Bridging veins traverse these villi and function as 1-way valves to drain blood from the neural tissue located beneath the innermost meningeal layer, the pia mater. Rupture of these thin-walled bridging veins under opposing mechanical forces releases blood into the subdural space, resulting in a subdural hematoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>A larger potential space between the dura mater and the brain, commonly observed in the developing brain or the aging brain due to cerebral contraction, allows cerebrospinal fluid to occupy a greater volume between the bridging veins. This expansion stretches the veins and increases vulnerability to rupture. Small extravasations may resolve spontaneously, whereas larger hemorrhages expand the subdural space, promote progressive blood layering around the brain, reduce intracranial volume, and ultimately precipitate herniation of cerebral structures.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>In pediatric patients, trauma represents the most common cause of subdural hematoma, with blunt and shearing forces serving as major contributing mechanisms. Use of cranium extraction devices and traumatic birth delivery accounts for a majority of subdural hematomas during the newborn period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r3">[3]</xref> Beyond the newborn stage and up to age 2 years, subdural hematoma most often results from accidental injury or intentional head trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Subdural hematoma caused by intentional injury commonly receives the designation &#x0201c;shaken baby syndrome.&#x0201d; This term reflects the repetitive back-and-forth motion of the brain within the skull, during which fragile bridging veins are subject to rupture from violent acceleration and deceleration forces acting against cerebral inertia (see <bold>Image</bold>. Shaken Baby Syndrome). Infants remain particularly vulnerable because insufficient neck muscle strength limits head stabilization during such events. Retinal hemorrhage frequently accompanies this mechanism and serves as a pathognomonic finding associated with shaken baby syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Subdural hematoma represents one of the intracranial injuries associated with abusive head trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref> Additional injuries identified in the setting of abusive head trauma include epidural hematoma, diffuse axonal injury, and parenchymal injury, among others. Epidemiological investigations categorize these findings under a unified classification of child abuse&#x02013;related injury. Data describing abusive head trauma demonstrate greater robustness in Europe and the United States than in other regions, with reported incidence rates reaching up to 17 per 100,000 child-years.</p>
        <p>The incidence of subdural hematoma in children younger than 2 years approaches 13 per 100,000 child-years. Risk increases further in children younger than 1 year, with a reported rate of 1:4761 in a study conducted in South Wales, England, corresponding to an incidence of up to 21 per 100,000 child-years in this age group. Developing countries report comparable rates, although available data remain limited. Gender distribution shows no difference during early childhood; however, accidental subdural hematoma related to trauma during adolescence demonstrates a predominance in males.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>In shaken baby syndrome or battered infant syndrome, violent shaking of the head generates rapid acceleration and deceleration forces that cause the brain to move in a direction opposite the meninges, leading to rupture of the bridging veins and bleeding into the subdural space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;This potential space can accumulate a substantial volume of blood at various stages, presenting as acute or subacute forms. Initial hemorrhage frequently remains unrecognized and later becomes evident as a chronic subdural hematoma.</p>
        <p>With sufficient blood accumulation occupying a large portion of the intracranial compartment, the cerebral midline shifts toward the contralateral side. This shift compresses brain structures against the inner surface of the calvarium, thereby reducing the volume of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles. Progressive limitation of intracranial space allows volumetric forces to displace the uncal portion of the temporal lobe toward the foramen magnum, resulting in cerebral herniation (see <bold>Image</bold>. Subdural Hematoma and Uncal Herniation).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histopathological examination of the clot surrounding the dura provides insight into the posttraumatic interval.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r6">[6]</xref> Determining this interval carries important medico-legal implications, particularly in cases of suspected child abuse, where the timing of the injury can assist in identifying a potential perpetrator. Examination focuses on the visualization of cellular components within the dura and the clot, including red blood cells, leukocytes, phagocytes, fibroblasts, hemosiderin-laden cells, fibrin, and proliferating blood vessels.</p>
        <p>Clots containing relatively intact red blood cells without significant differentiation or evidence of lysis indicate an acute phase. When the subdural clot demonstrates organization with fibrin deposition, macrophages laden with hemosiderin, and lysed red blood cells at the center, the clot represents a subacute stage. These histopathological findings remain interpretive and rarely provide definitive timing, and their assessment is generally reserved for forensic postmortem examinations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>History-taking and physical examination in infants and children can present significant challenges, particularly in cases of suspected abusive head trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinicians may encounter injuries that conflict with the caretaker&#x02019;s account, and any suspicion should prompt a thorough clinical evaluation and notification of the appropriate authorities responsible for child protection. Newborns who experience traumatic birth often present with a history of difficult passage through the birth canal. Particular attention must be paid to the use of forceps or vacuum extraction devices during delivery, as these interventions can contribute to intracranial injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Caretakers and family members frequently report abnormal breathing patterns, apneic episodes, behavioral changes, vomiting, abnormal movements, or seizures in patients with subdural hematomas. Careful observation may reveal subtle or overt alterations in mental status, ranging from irritability to lethargy. When trauma history is apparent, external scalp injuries or palpable cephalohematomas may be present. In infants with an open fontanel, palpation may demonstrate a tense, noncompliant, or bulging fontanel. Other signs of abusive trauma may appear on the skin as bruising or limb deformities. Occasionally, physical examination may reveal no visible external injury, emphasizing the importance of careful assessment and vigilance.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Management of subdural hematomas should include a complete blood count (CBC) with careful attention to hemoglobin and hematocrit levels. Significant accumulation of blood in the subdural space can lead to anemia from blood loss before neurological signs become apparent. In cases of longstanding accumulation, as seen in chronic subdural hematoma, platelet counts often decrease, indicating the onset of consumptive coagulopathy. Prolonged prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time, decreased fibrinogen, and elevated fibrin degradation products can confirm this condition.&#x000a0;A basic metabolic panel (BMP) provides additional insight, with particular focus on serum potassium, as hyperkalemia reflects red blood cell breakdown within the subdural space. Stabilizing the patient to reduce the risk of secondary injury remains a critical priority.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Diagnostic Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>Following initial stabilization, radiological evaluation with computerized tomography (CT) of the head proves essential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;When time allows, and sedation risks are minimized, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain offers more detailed visualization, including subtle changes in the brain and brainstem (see <bold>Images</bold>. Brain Trauma Computed Tomography and Subdural Hematoma). An initial MRI can also assist in estimating the age of the subdural hematoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Immediate management of a subdural hematoma begins with securing the airway, supporting breathing, and stabilizing circulation, tasks that fall within the critical care team&#x02019;s primary responsibilities. Following initial stabilization and close monitoring, a secondary care plan should be implemented. This plan requires consultation with neurosurgery and neurology to reach consensus on the injury and to determine both immediate and long-term consequences for the patient.</p>
        <p>Conservative, nonsurgical management may be appropriate for subacute and chronic subdural hematomas when blood accumulation has not progressed sufficiently to compress the brain or brainstem. In contrast, a rapidly expanding subdural hematoma or one causing signs of increased intracranial pressure,&#x000a0;eg, hypertension, bradycardia with irregular respirations, demands urgent surgical evacuation to preserve vital functions. Concurrently, clinicians should initiate immediate medical management, including sedation, neuromuscular blockade when indicated, moderate hyperventilation targeting a PaCO<sub>2</sub> of 32 to 36 mm Hg, maintenance of SpO<sub>2</sub> above 95%, head elevation, and avoidance of hyperthermia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Infusion of hypertonic saline or mannitol helps reduce intracranial pressure by inducing osmotic shifts within the brain and transiently modifying cerebral blood flow rheology, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Other fluid substances may collect within the layers of the meninges, giving the appearance of a subdural hematoma. A careful history and evaluation of&#x000a0;radiological findings are&#x000a0;necessary to rule out a purulent accumulation&#x000a0;in&#x000a0;the subdural&#x000a0;abscess. In some instances, the subarachnoid space occupied by the commonly occurring cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) with a natively decreased brain mass, as in hydrocephalus ex vacuo, may give the appearance of a subdural hematoma.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of children affected by subdural hematoma is widely variable and depends on the extent of the intracranial injury. Many cases of subdural hematoma, either traumatic or abusive, remain undetected. However, many children live with severe neurological deficits, including seizures, neurodevelopmental delay with static encephalopathy, because of severe, devastating neurological injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Subdural hematoma can lead to a wide range of acute and long-term complications. In the acute phase, ongoing bleeding and cerebral edema may cause rising intracranial pressure, herniation, and potentially fatal neurologic decline if not recognized and managed promptly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;These risks are higher when additional traumatic brain injuries (eg, diffuse axonal injury or parenchymal contusions) are present.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r11">[11]</xref> Hydrocephalus is another important complication in pediatric traumatic brain injury. Children with subdural hematoma are particularly vulnerable and may require CSF diversion and continued neurosurgical follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Posttraumatic seizures are also common, especially in children with severe injuries, depressed mental status, or intracranial hemorrhage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Seizures may occur early (within the first week following injury) or later in recovery and can contribute to secondary injury and long-term epilepsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Some patients develop chronic or acute-on-chronic subdural collections after the initial event, particularly those with coagulopathies, bleeding diathesis, or repeated trauma. These may present weeks to months later with new neurologic symptoms, seizures, or recurrent signs of increased intracranial pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>In the long term, survivors (especially infants and young children with abusive head trauma) may experience persistent neurocognitive, behavioral, visual, and motor impairments, including developmental delay, spasticity, learning difficulties, and static encephalopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;These outcomes highlight the importance of early rehabilitation, coordinated follow-up, and ongoing&#x000a0;interprofessional support for patients and their families.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>The medical team should consider a&#x000a0;neurosurgical consultation early in the management of a patient with a subdural hematoma. A pediatric neurologist is also an essential addition to the consulting physicians. An ophthalmology consultation with a complete assessment of the retina is vital to determine abusive trauma in children.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence and patient education are key to reducing the risk of subdural hematoma, especially in infants and young children. Caregivers should receive clear guidance on normal infant crying and safe soothing techniques, with an emphasis that shaking an infant can cause severe brain injury or death. Anticipatory guidance at well-child visits and community programs that address caregiver stress and support needs are important in preventing abusive head trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinicians should also reinforce safe sleep and handling practices, proper car seat use, and strategies to reduce fall-related injuries.</p>
        <p>For families of children who have already sustained a subdural hematoma, education should focus on recognizing signs of worsening neurologic status (eg, seizures, persistent vomiting, or changes in behavior) and when to seek urgent care. Caregivers should understand the need for follow-up with neurosurgery, neurology, and rehabilitation services, adherence to medications, and early developmental support when indicated. When abusive head trauma is involved, social workers and child protection teams play a central role in ensuring safety and coordinating ongoing services.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r25">[25]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>&#x000a0;Factors that should be kept in mind when managing subdural hematomas include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>In infants and young children, any subdural hematoma warrants evaluation for abusive head trauma, including detailed history, skeletal survey, and ophthalmologic examination when indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Absence of external trauma does not rule out significant intracranial injury; symptoms may be limited to irritability, lethargy, poor feeding, or brief apnea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>CT is the preferred initial imaging study in the acute setting; MRI is superior for evaluating parenchymal injury, subtle hemorrhages, and dating of blood products.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r12">[12]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic or acute-on-chronic subdural hematomas may present weeks after the initial event and require a high index of suspicion to diagnose.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r18">[18]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early neurosurgical consultation is recommended even for small or seemingly stable collections, as deterioration can occur rapidly, especially in infants and older adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>When abusive head trauma is suspected, prompt involvement of social work and child protection services is essential to ensure safety and coordinate ongoing support.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r25">[25]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Subdural hematoma represents a serious intracranial injury resulting from bleeding between the dura mater and the brain, commonly caused by trauma, including abusive head trauma in infants and young children. Subdural hematoma can present acutely, subacutely, or chronically, with severity ranging from asymptomatic minor bleeding to life-threatening increases in intracranial pressure, herniation, and long-term neurocognitive impairment. Pediatric populations, particularly neonates and infants, are highly vulnerable due to fragile bridging veins, incomplete skull development, and limited neck muscle strength. Prompt recognition, accurate diagnosis through imaging and laboratory evaluation, and timely intervention&#x02014;whether medical stabilization or neurosurgical evacuation&#x02014;remain critical to preventing secondary injury and optimizing outcomes.</p>
        <p>Management of patients with&#x000a0;subdural hematoma requires coordinated interprofessional care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29623.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Physicians and advanced practitioners lead the evaluation, stabilization, and decision-making regarding surgical or conservative treatment. Nurses provide continuous monitoring, assess neurological status, and implement interventions such as sedation, head elevation, and hyperosmolar therapy. Pharmacists ensure safe and effective medication management, including osmotic agents and seizure prophylaxis. Collaboration with neurosurgeons, radiologists, and rehabilitation specialists ensures timely imaging, procedural intervention, and long-term follow-up. Effective communication, early identification of abusive head trauma, and coordinated care planning enhance patient safety, optimize recovery, and improve team performance in high-acuity pediatric settings.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29623.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29623&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29623">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://mdsearchlight.com/neurology/subdural-hematoma-shaken-baby-syndrome-brain-bleed/?utm_source=pubmedlink&#x00026;utm_campaign=MDS&#x00026;utm_content=29623">Click here for a simplified version.</ext-link>
</p>
