    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-29623.s18">
        <fig id="article-29623.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Subdural Hematoma Tentorium. Rupture of thin-walled bridging veins under opposing mechanical forces releases blood into the subdural space, resulting in a subdural hematoma. Contributed by S Dulebohn, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="SDH__tentorium" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-29623.s19">
        <fig id="article-29623.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Subdural Hematoma. A subdural hematoma develops from the accumulation of blood beneath the dura mater, one of the protective layers covering the brain tissue under the calvarium. Contributed by S Dulebohn, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="subdural__hematoma" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-29623.s20">
        <fig id="article-29623.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Brain Trauma Computed Tomography. This image shows a preoperative computed tomography (CT) scan of a patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 14, demonstrating the following findings: Emergent CT imaging revealed a sagittally oriented skull fracture extending from the vertex to the foramen magnum, as well as a transverse parietal and temporal bone fracture. Multiple frontal, parietal, and temporal lobe contusions with associated interhemispheric hemorrhage and a left-sided subdural hematoma measuring 1.7 mm in greatest depth were appreciated. Effacement of the basilar cisterns was noted without shift of midline structures. T Rehman, R Ali, I Tawil, H Yonas, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brain_trauma_CT.jpg">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Brain_trauma_CT" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-29623.s21">
        <fig id="article-29623.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Subdural Hematoma and Uncal Herniation. Progressive limitation of intracranial space allows volumetric forces to displace the uncal portion of the temporal lobe toward the foramen magnum, resulting in cerebral herniation. Contributed by&#x000a0;S Bhimji, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="uncal" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-29623.s22">
        <fig id="article-29623.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Shaken Baby Syndrome. Magnetic resonance imaging in a 3-month-old girl with chronic subdural hematoma (SDH). (A) The T1-weighted image shows predominantly low-signal SDH with a high-signal focus in the left frontal region. (B) The T2-weighted image shows predominantly high signal intensity, with a focal area of low intensity. (C) Contrast-enhanced T1-weighted image demonstrates linear dural enhancement. The National Center for Biotechnology Information (MRI by The Korean Radiological Society; http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0)</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="kjr-2-171-g001" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
